#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
msgid "B<archinstall> supports plugins via two methods."
msgstr "B<archinstall> acceptă module prin două metode."

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
msgid ""
"First method is directly via the B<--plugin> parameter when running as a CLI "
"tool. This will load a specific plugin locally or remotely via a path."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
msgid ""
"The second method is via Python\\(aqs built in \\X'tty: link https://"
"packaging.python.org/en/latest/specifications/entry-points/'I<\\%plugin "
"discovery>\\X'tty: link' using \\X'tty: link https://docs.python.org/3/"
"library/importlib.metadata.html#entry-points'I<\\%entry points>\\X'tty: "
"link' categorized as B<archinstall.plugin>\\&."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: archlinux
#, no-wrap
msgid "--plugin parameter"
msgstr "--plugin parametru"

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
msgid ""
"The parameter has the benefit of being stored in the B<--conf> state, "
"meaning when re-running an installation — the plugin will automatically be "
"loaded.  It\\(aqs limitation is that it requires an initial path to be known "
"and written and be cumbersome."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: archlinux
#, no-wrap
msgid "Plugin Discovery"
msgstr "Detectarea modulelor"

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
msgid ""
"This method allows for multiple plugins to be loaded with the drawback that "
"they have to be installed beforehand on the system running "
"B<archinstall>\\&.  This mainly targets those who build their own ISO\\(aqs "
"and package specific setups for their needs."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: archlinux
#, no-wrap
msgid "What\\(aqs supported?"
msgstr "Ce este acceptat?"

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
msgid ""
"Currently the documentation for this is scarse. Until that is resolved, the "
"best way to find supported features is to search the source code for "
"\\X'tty: link https://github.com/search?q=repo%3Aarchlinux%2Farchinstall+"
"%22plugin.on_%22&type=code'I<\\%plugin.on_>\\X'tty: link' as this will give "
"a clear indication of which calls are made to plugins."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: archlinux
#, no-wrap
msgid "How does it work?"
msgstr "Cum funcționează acesta?"

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
msgid ""
"B<archinstall> plugins use a discovery-driven approach where plugins are "
"queried for certain functions.  As an example, if a plugin has the following "
"function:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"def on_pacstrap(*packages):\n"
"    ...\n"
msgstr ""
"def on_pacstrap(*packages):\n"
"    ...\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
msgid ""
"The function B<archinstall.Pacman().strap([\"some packages\"])> is hardcoded "
"to iterate plugins and look for B<on_pacstrap> in the plugin.  If the "
"function exists, B<\\&.strap()> will call the plugin\\(aqs function and "
"replace the initial package list with the result from the plugin."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
msgid ""
"The best way to document these calls is currently undecided, as it\\(aqs "
"hard to document this behavior dynamically."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: archlinux
#, no-wrap
msgid "Writing your own?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
msgid ""
"The simplest way currently is to look at a reference implementation or the "
"community. Two of these are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
msgid ""
"\\X'tty: link https://github.com/torxed/archinstall-aur'I<\\%torxed/"
"archinstall-aur>\\X'tty: link'"
msgstr ""
"\\X'tty: link https://github.com/torxed/archinstall-aur'I<\\%torxed/"
"archinstall-aur>\\X'tty: link'"

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
msgid ""
"\\X'tty: link https://github.com/phisch/archinstall-aur'I<\\%phisch/"
"archinstall-aur>\\X'tty: link'"
msgstr ""
"\\X'tty: link https://github.com/phisch/archinstall-aur'I<\\%phisch/"
"archinstall-aur>\\X'tty: link'"

#. type: Plain text
#: archlinux
msgid ""
"And search for \\X'tty: link https://github.com/search?"
"q=repo%3Aarchlinux%2Farchinstall+%22plugin.on_%22&type=code'I<\\"
"%plugin.on_>\\X'tty: link' in the code base to find what B<archinstall> will "
"look for. PR\\(aqs are welcome to widen the support for this."
msgstr ""

